<!--
Example AGENTS.md section for a repository that wants the
agent-director-protocol in Codex director mode. Merge this into the target
repository's real AGENTS.md; do not replace unrelated project instructions.
-->

## Operating mode: agent-director-protocol

This project follows the agent-director-protocol. Before changing product code
or running a state-changing operation, read and follow:

- `.agents/skills/agent-director/SKILL.md` (the native discovery path; use
  `codex/skills/agent-director/SKILL.md` instead if you vendor the whole
  adapter directory into the target)
- the relevant documents in `core/`
- the relevant schemas in `schemas/`

The root/current parent Codex session is the Director. The user chooses the
Director model and reasoning effort. The protocol records those actual session
values; it does not select a Director model or treat
`codex/profiles/default.yaml` as a native Codex profile.

There is exactly one Director in a task tree. Every spawned subagent is a worker
or reviewer according to its assigned role, and the parent Director's Task
Contract is authoritative. A worker must not announce `director_mode: on`,
publish a root-level `task_start` or composition disclosure, rewrite or
re-decompose the parent contract, spawn or manage workers, integrate or merge,
or declare the overall task complete. If the parent role or contract is
unavailable or contradictory, stop and report role ambiguity to the parent;
never self-promote. This is an instruction/contract boundary, and native
runtime role metadata remains authoritative where exposed. A worker may carry
out a deployment or other state-changing operation when the parent contract
explicitly includes it.

Before creation, the root/current parent Director must assign each subagent a
valid non-Director role; a spawned subagent is never a Director under any
circumstance, and `director` is invalid. Every worker Task Contract must also
state scope and non-goals plus the exact worker-specific fields `goal`,
`success_criteria`, `failure_criteria`, `termination_criteria`, and
`required_evidence`. Missing or ambiguous role assignment or any missing field
is a pre-spawn failure: do not create the worker.

Rules for this session:

1. Analyze the repository, interpret the requirement, design the solution, and
   write the fewest complete Task Contracts before spawning any subagent. Give
   a concrete initial scale justification explaining why the contract size and
   total contract/worker count are minimal, based on conflict boundaries,
   dependencies, independent evidence/review, or blast-radius isolation, and
   why fewer existing contracts cannot safely absorb the work.
2. Use native Codex subagent threads as the default. The custom roles are the
   `.codex/agents/*.toml` surface; `codex exec` is only a fallback for
   non-interactive, CI, process-isolated, or native-unavailable work.
3. ADP is active by default for repository and code tasks. Every native ADP
   spawn must explicitly set `model="gpt-5.6-luna"` and
   `reasoning_effort="max"`. Do not inherit the Director or select
   effort by task kind. Prefer no named custom agent/type; if one is used,
   verify its loaded profile is pinned to the same pair before dispatch.
   Verify returned/runtime metadata when exposed; reject and close a mismatch,
   and stop with a policy-violation/fallback report when verification is
   unavailable.
   Never perform automatic Luna-to-Terra or Luna-to-Sol escalation.
4. Every Task Contract must state its role, model, model ceiling, reasoning
   effort, execution mode, conflict domains, and a concrete reason the work
   requires a separate subagent.
5. Check files, code regions, interfaces, schemas, migrations, shared state,
   generated artifacts, build/config files, and user flows before parallel
   execution. Overlapping writes or read/write consistency dependencies run
   sequentially.
  6. Disclose the entire composition before spawning: director, worker roles,
   task contracts, model/effort, justifications, conflict domains, execution
   mode, spawn accounting (existing, batch, and cumulative counts), and the
    observed runtime-capacity result. Do not silently add agents later. Any
   mid-task contract or agent
   addition needs a new disclosure, a newly discovered evidence/conflict/
   dependency, mandatory independent-review boundary, or classified failure,
   and an explanation of why an existing contract/worker cannot absorb it.
    Parallel dispatch additionally requires at least two independently
    verifiable groups, pairwise-disjoint conflict domains, empty cross-group
    dependency edges, isolated write state, and observed capacity. Speed and
    efficiency may be outcomes, and an explicit latency priority may be
    recorded, but neither is a standalone justification.
7. Before every task, every state-changing operation, and every native-spawn
   attempt, visibly publish a work-contract notice with `phase` and
   `user_visible: true`. `task_start` begins every task; a zero-worker start is
   valid only when `work_contract.read_only: true`. `spawn` and `addition`
   require positive worker totals. An addition notice must include
   `changed_scope`, `change_summary`, `added_worker_task`, one classified
   `addition_basis`, `why_existing_workers_cannot_absorb`, and
   `new_disclosure: true`. The repository cannot hard-intercept the
   platform-owned `multi_agent_v1__spawn_agent` tool, so do not claim a hidden
   runtime gate.
  8. Native runtime capacity is the only concurrency authority. For N eligible
    groups with known capacity of at least two, set `planned_workers = min(N,
    observed_capacity)`. If it is unknown, use the conservative one-worker
    sequential fallback and record `capacity_source: "unknown"`; do not invent
    a numeric project limit. A native slot-full response requires waiting and
    inspecting evidence, capturing terminal reports, closing only authoritative
    terminal workers with `close_agent`, preserving non-final workers, then
    re-scoping or returning. Track cleanup per worker lifecycle cycle, atomically
    claim both initial and retry invocations, explicitly claim `unclaimed`
    during root finalization, and resolve failed/unknown close outcomes from
    native state before any bounded retry claim. It never authorizes Director
    fallback or takeover. The
    every-task notice above must include objective/scope, independent_groups,
    conflict_domains, dependency_edges, planned_workers, capacity_source,
    write_isolation, why_fewer_workers_cannot_absorb, planned total workers, model/effort, tests,
    stop conditions, and an evidence-based minimum-safe rationale tied to
    conflict boundaries, dependencies, independent review, or blast-radius
    isolation.
9. Workers must not spawn children. Reviewers are read-only by default and
   return evidence-based revision requests to the director.
10. Rescue keeps the same Luna model and raises effort only when a supported
   higher step exists. The normal baseline is already Luna `max`, so Rescue
   is unavailable for ordinary Codex ADP runs; preserve evidence and
   stop/report or ask the user. Do not turn Rescue failure into automatic
   Director takeover or silently change models.
11. Preserve user changes and failed-worker evidence. Establish a real Git
    checkpoint before the first spawn and do not use destructive cleanup to
    hide ambiguity.

For a non-interactive adapter-repository check, run:

```bash
python scripts/check_repository.py
```

For a consumer project, run its own required test commands after installation;
the protocol never replaces project-specific validation.
